WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.245
Lesson 2.7.

00:00:02.245 --> 00:00:05.020
Advanced AMM design and LVR.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:08.237
By the end, you'll know the major AMM designs,

00:00:08.237 --> 00:00:12.275
and measure an LP's real cost with loss-versus-rebalancing.

00:00:13.575 --> 00:00:14.865
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.865 --> 00:00:17.322
The major A.M.M. designs, and which

00:00:17.322 --> 00:00:19.533
pair each one actually fits.

00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:23.954
Loss-versus-rebalancing, L.V.R., the more complete measure of

00:00:23.954 --> 00:00:27.178
what an L.P. actually loses to arbitrage.

00:00:27.178 --> 00:00:30.893
A full worked example, with real numbers, showing exactly

00:00:30.893 --> 00:00:33.948
when fees beat L.V.R., and when they don't.

00:00:33.948 --> 00:00:36.643
And what changes once volatility rises.

00:00:37.943 --> 00:00:39.768
The major AMM designs.

00:00:41.068 --> 00:00:42.687
Start with the two ends of the

00:00:42.687 --> 00:00:45.912
spectrum, since they solve genuinely different problems.

00:00:45.912 --> 00:00:49.366
Constant product, x times y equals k, from Lesson

00:00:49.366 --> 00:00:52.268
two point two, works for any pair at all,

00:00:52.268 --> 00:00:56.367
spreading liquidity evenly across every possible price.

00:00:56.367 --> 00:01:00.442
StableSwap is built specifically for assets that should trade

00:01:00.442 --> 00:01:05.175
near one-to-one, stablecoins, or a liquid-staking token against E.T.H.,

00:01:05.175 --> 00:01:08.724
blending a flat, low-slippage curve near the peg with

00:01:08.724 --> 00:01:11.682
constant-product safety further away from it.

00:01:12.981 --> 00:01:15.702
Here's exactly how StableSwap achieves that

00:01:15.702 --> 00:01:18.476
low slippage near the peg, mechanically.

00:01:18.476 --> 00:01:21.197
Near the peg, the curve behaves almost flat,

00:01:21.197 --> 00:01:24.643
like a constant-sum pool, meaning trades near one-to-one

00:01:24.643 --> 00:01:26.696
barely move the price at all.

00:01:26.696 --> 00:01:28.659
Further from the peg, it blends

00:01:28.659 --> 00:01:32.271
toward constant-product behaviour instead, for safety.

00:01:32.271 --> 00:01:35.536
And an amplification parameter tunes exactly how

00:01:35.536 --> 00:01:38.602
wide that flat, low-slippage zone actually is.

00:01:39.902 --> 00:01:42.259
Two more designs worth knowing by name,

00:01:42.259 --> 00:01:45.092
each solving a specific, different problem.

00:01:45.092 --> 00:01:48.296
Weighted pools hold more than two tokens, or uneven

00:01:48.296 --> 00:01:52.916
weights like eighty-twenty, and an eighty-twenty pool specifically carries

00:01:52.916 --> 00:01:56.297
less impermanent loss on its eighty-percent token.

00:01:56.297 --> 00:01:59.613
Concentrated liquidity lets you place a position only

00:01:59.613 --> 00:02:02.438
within a price range you choose, tracking the

00:02:02.438 --> 00:02:05.448
square root of price internally, covered in more

00:02:05.448 --> 00:02:08.027
depth as your positions get more advanced.

00:02:09.327 --> 00:02:13.078
Zoom into concentrated liquidity specifically, since “a

00:02:13.078 --> 00:02:15.422
chosen price range” is easy to nod

00:02:15.422 --> 00:02:18.267
along to without seeing the mechanism.

00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:20.629
You choose a price range, called a tick

00:02:20.629 --> 00:02:23.587
range, rather than the entire possible curve.

00:02:23.587 --> 00:02:26.301
Your capital only becomes active liquidity

00:02:26.301 --> 00:02:28.810
while price sits inside that range.

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:31.140
Inside it, you earn a far larger share

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:34.068
of fees per dollar deposited, since your capital

00:02:34.068 --> 00:02:37.295
isn't spread thin across prices that never trade.

00:02:37.295 --> 00:02:39.518
And if price moves outside your range

00:02:39.518 --> 00:02:42.268
entirely, your position earns nothing until it

00:02:42.268 --> 00:02:44.140
moves back in, or you adjust it.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:46.530
Quick check.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.219
Why is StableSwap specifically built for

00:02:49.219 --> 00:02:51.645
near-1:1 pairs, rather than any pair?

00:02:56.065 --> 00:02:59.551
The answer: it blends a flat, low-slippage curve near

00:02:59.551 --> 00:03:03.619
the peg with constant-product safety further away, giving very

00:03:03.619 --> 00:03:07.105
low slippage exactly where these pairs actually trade.

00:03:08.405 --> 00:03:09.890
Loss-versus-rebalancing.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:14.620
Here's precisely what L.V.R. measures, and why it's

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:18.020
more complete than impermanent loss on its own.

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:20.283
A pool's price only updates when an

00:03:20.283 --> 00:03:23.425
arbitrageur actually trades against it, after the

00:03:23.425 --> 00:03:26.190
real price has already moved elsewhere, the

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:29.130
exact mechanism from Lesson two point two.

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:32.995
L.V.R. measures what that lag costs L.P.s, compared

00:03:32.995 --> 00:03:37.232
against a portfolio that rebalances continuously, at the

00:03:37.232 --> 00:03:40.205
real market price, the instant it moves.

00:03:41.505 --> 00:03:43.339
Here's the formula itself, for

00:03:43.339 --> 00:03:46.480
a full-range constant-product pool specifically.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:49.490
Take sigma, the token's annual volatility.

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:50.480
Square it.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:51.875
Divide by eight.

00:03:51.875 --> 00:03:54.263
That gives L.V.R., as a percentage

00:03:54.263 --> 00:03:56.200
of pool value, per year.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:59.008
And the rule that actually matters in practice:

00:03:59.008 --> 00:04:01.347
your fees have to beat this number, not

00:04:01.347 --> 00:04:03.570
just beat impermanent loss on its own.

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:07.685
Here's the actual shape of that relationship.

00:04:07.685 --> 00:04:11.723
Because volatility is squared in the formula, L.V.R. doesn't

00:04:11.723 --> 00:04:15.165
rise in a straight line as volatility increases; it

00:04:15.165 --> 00:04:19.202
rises faster and faster, the same bending-curve pattern from

00:04:19.202 --> 00:04:22.843
the price-impact formula in Lesson two point two, just

00:04:22.843 --> 00:04:26.020
applied to volatility instead of trade size now.

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:30.362
Worth reconciling this with Lesson two point five

00:04:30.362 --> 00:04:33.887
directly, since it can sound contradictory otherwise.

00:04:33.887 --> 00:04:36.756
Arbitrage M.E.V. is harmless to the market

00:04:36.756 --> 00:04:39.159
as a whole; it's exactly what keeps

00:04:39.159 --> 00:04:42.528
a pool's price honest against everywhere else.

00:04:42.528 --> 00:04:46.386
L.V.R. is the same arbitrage, viewed specifically from

00:04:46.386 --> 00:04:49.332
the L.P.'s side of the trade; harmless to

00:04:49.332 --> 00:04:52.348
the system, and a real, measurable cost to

00:04:52.348 --> 00:04:54.943
whoever was supplying that liquidity.

00:04:56.244 --> 00:04:57.334
Quick check.

00:04:57.334 --> 00:04:59.659
What does LVR actually measure, that a

00:04:59.659 --> 00:05:02.939
simple impermanent-loss snapshot doesn't fully capture?

00:05:07.359 --> 00:05:11.580
The answer: what LPs lose to arbitrageurs specifically because

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:15.331
the pool's price lags the real market, compared against

00:05:15.331 --> 00:05:19.284
a portfolio that rebalances continuously at the real price.

00:05:20.584 --> 00:05:25.168
One practical consequence worth naming before the worked example:

00:05:25.168 --> 00:05:29.335
concentrated liquidity and weighted pools both carry a form

00:05:29.335 --> 00:05:33.663
of L.V.R. too, not just full-range constant-product pools.

00:05:33.663 --> 00:05:36.522
The exact formula changes shape for each

00:05:36.522 --> 00:05:39.799
design, but the underlying cause is identical:

00:05:39.799 --> 00:05:42.797
price lags the market until an arbitrageur

00:05:42.797 --> 00:05:45.607
closes the gap, at the LP's expense.

00:05:45.607 --> 00:05:47.639
Full-range is simply the case with

00:05:47.639 --> 00:05:49.672
the cleanest, most citable formula.

00:05:50.972 --> 00:05:51.977
Worked example.

00:05:53.277 --> 00:05:56.037
Here's the arithmetic, at eighty percent

00:05:56.037 --> 00:05:58.962
annual E.T.H. volatility, step by step.

00:05:58.962 --> 00:06:01.462
Square sigma: zero point eight squared

00:06:01.462 --> 00:06:03.237
is zero point six four.

00:06:03.237 --> 00:06:06.599
Divide by eight: zero point six four divided by

00:06:06.599 --> 00:06:10.332
eight is zero point zero eight, or eight percent.

00:06:10.332 --> 00:06:13.266
That's your L.V.R., as a share of pool value,

00:06:13.266 --> 00:06:16.137
every year, before any fees are even counted.

00:06:17.437 --> 00:06:19.673
Now bring fees into it, at a twelve

00:06:19.673 --> 00:06:22.272
percent fee A.P.R. on this same pool.

00:06:22.272 --> 00:06:24.217
Fees: twelve percent a year.

00:06:24.217 --> 00:06:26.662
L.V.R.: eight percent a year.

00:06:26.662 --> 00:06:29.626
The difference, roughly four percent a year,

00:06:29.626 --> 00:06:32.327
is what L.P.s actually keep, before gas,

00:06:32.327 --> 00:06:34.867
at this specific volatility level.

00:06:34.867 --> 00:06:37.142
Not the fee A.P.R. alone.

00:06:37.142 --> 00:06:39.657
The fee A.P.R., minus L.V.R.

00:06:40.957 --> 00:06:44.145
Now raise volatility to 110%, the same pair,

00:06:44.145 --> 00:06:47.120
a more turbulent year, and watch what the

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:50.962
squared relationship does to this exact same math.

00:06:50.962 --> 00:06:53.957
1.1 squared is 1.21.

00:06:53.957 --> 00:06:57.238
Divided by 8, that's roughly 15% LVR a

00:06:57.238 --> 00:07:01.275
year, nearly double the previous figure, from a

00:07:01.275 --> 00:07:04.472
volatility increase of only about 38%.

00:07:05.772 --> 00:07:08.114
And here's exactly what that does to

00:07:08.114 --> 00:07:10.645
the same twelve percent fee A.P.R. that

00:07:10.645 --> 00:07:12.717
was profitable a moment ago.

00:07:12.717 --> 00:07:15.860
Twelve percent in fees, minus roughly fifteen

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:19.002
percent in L.V.R., comes out negative, around

00:07:19.002 --> 00:07:21.147
minus three percent a year.

00:07:21.147 --> 00:07:24.507
The same fee income that comfortably beat L.V.R.

00:07:24.507 --> 00:07:28.279
at eighty percent volatility now loses money outright,

00:07:28.279 --> 00:07:31.502
at one hundred ten percent, with nothing about

00:07:31.502 --> 00:07:33.902
the fees themselves having changed.

00:07:35.202 --> 00:07:38.803
Here's how to actually run this comparison yourself, on

00:07:38.803 --> 00:07:42.597
any pool, using this program's own calculator tool, rather

00:07:42.597 --> 00:07:45.727
than doing the arithmetic by hand each time.

00:07:45.727 --> 00:07:48.488
Estimate the token's annual volatility, from

00:07:48.488 --> 00:07:50.567
its own recent price history.

00:07:50.567 --> 00:07:53.870
Feed that volatility, and the pool's advertised

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:56.992
fee A.P.R., into the calculator directly.

00:07:56.992 --> 00:08:01.111
And only proceed if fee A.P.R. genuinely clears L.V.R.,

00:08:01.111 --> 00:08:04.127
with real room to spare, not just barely.

00:08:05.427 --> 00:08:06.517
Quick check.

00:08:06.517 --> 00:08:08.406
Volatility is sixty percent a

00:08:08.406 --> 00:08:10.407
year, on a full-range pool.

00:08:10.407 --> 00:08:12.182
What's the approximate LVR?

00:08:16.602 --> 00:08:19.647
The answer: about four point five percent a year.

00:08:19.647 --> 00:08:23.150
Zero point six squared is zero point three six,

00:08:23.150 --> 00:08:26.507
divided by eight is zero point zero four five.

00:08:27.807 --> 00:08:29.482
Hooks, and new contract risk.

00:08:30.782 --> 00:08:33.297
Worth knowing this one newer category by

00:08:33.297 --> 00:08:35.873
name, since it changes what due diligence

00:08:35.873 --> 00:08:37.707
on a pool actually means.

00:08:37.707 --> 00:08:40.352
Hooks let a pool run custom code at the

00:08:40.352 --> 00:08:44.122
exact moment of every swap: dynamic fees, built-in limit

00:08:44.122 --> 00:08:48.522
orders, live oracle feeds, whatever the pool's designer wrote.

00:08:48.522 --> 00:08:51.019
Each hook is its own additional smart

00:08:51.019 --> 00:08:54.107
contract, carrying its own additional risk, on

00:08:54.107 --> 00:08:56.472
top of the AMM design underneath it.

00:08:57.772 --> 00:08:59.232
Here's your checklist.

00:08:59.232 --> 00:09:01.026
Do it now, before choosing a

00:09:01.026 --> 00:09:03.367
pool type or depositing into one.

00:09:03.367 --> 00:09:06.474
Choose pool type by the actual pair: StableSwap

00:09:06.474 --> 00:09:10.851
for pegged pairs, weighted or concentrated liquidity otherwise.

00:09:10.851 --> 00:09:14.809
Compare fee A.P.R. against L.V.R. specifically, not

00:09:14.809 --> 00:09:17.621
just impermanent loss on its own.

00:09:17.621 --> 00:09:20.270
And review any hook contract the exact same

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:23.039
way you'd review any other contract, since it

00:09:23.039 --> 00:09:25.206
carries the exact same kind of risk.

00:09:26.506 --> 00:09:29.484
One last honest framing, before the recap, since

00:09:29.484 --> 00:09:33.130
“expert” shouldn't mean “ignore this.” Nothing here changes

00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:36.168
what you learned about impermanent loss in Lesson

00:09:36.168 --> 00:09:38.842
two point four; L.V.R. simply gives you the

00:09:38.842 --> 00:09:41.880
sharper, more complete lens for judging whether a

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:45.223
specific pool's fees genuinely compensate for the risk

00:09:45.223 --> 00:09:48.261
you're taking on, especially as volatility climbs.

00:09:49.561 --> 00:09:50.806
Let's recap.

00:09:50.806 --> 00:09:54.669
Constant product fits any pair; StableSwap fits near-1:1

00:09:54.669 --> 00:09:58.426
pairs specifically, with much lower slippage there.

00:09:58.426 --> 00:10:02.664
Weighted pools handle uneven splits; concentrated liquidity

00:10:02.664 --> 00:10:05.718
focuses a position into a chosen range.

00:10:05.718 --> 00:10:09.757
L.V.R. equals volatility squared, divided by eight, for a

00:10:09.757 --> 00:10:13.586
full-range pool, and it's the more complete measure of

00:10:13.586 --> 00:10:17.973
what arbitrage actually costs L.P.s. And because volatility is

00:10:17.973 --> 00:10:22.359
squared, L.V.R. rises far faster than volatility itself, which

00:10:22.359 --> 00:10:26.537
is exactly why higher-volatility pairs need much higher fee

00:10:26.537 --> 00:10:28.278
tiers to stay profitable.

00:10:29.579 --> 00:10:33.004
Compare fee APR against LVR, not just impermanent

00:10:33.004 --> 00:10:36.729
loss, before trusting any pool's advertised yield.

00:10:36.729 --> 00:10:40.444
Next up, Lesson 2.8: The MEV supply chain.
